Subject: Hand back the spare pages of the stream buffer once a transfer has been sent

Each guest thread's stream to the host renderer (HvSocketStream, 0001) keeps one buffer for
everything it sends, and the buffer grows to the largest transfer the thread ever made: a
picture a CPU wrote (a 2880x2880 one is 32 MiB), a large texture, a big command batch. Its pages
then stay resident in the process for as long as it runs, though nothing reads them again
before the next transfer writes them. Once a transfer has been written to the socket, the whole
pages past the buffer's first 256 KiB are marked free (dsa_spare_pages.h): the kernel takes them
when the guest reclaims page cache, and a transfer written before then reuses them without a
fault.

Applies after 0001 on gfxstream d047a57228332d995d36600792fa9ccc26cf8ae6.
---
diff --git a/guest/OpenglSystemCommon/HvSocketStream.cpp b/guest/OpenglSystemCommon/HvSocketStream.cpp
--- a/guest/OpenglSystemCommon/HvSocketStream.cpp
+++ b/guest/OpenglSystemCommon/HvSocketStream.cpp
@@ -12,6 +12,8 @@
 #include <unistd.h>

 #include <cutils/log.h>
+
+#include "dsa_spare_pages.h"

 HvSocketStream::HvSocketStream(size_t buffer_size, uint32_t port)
     : IOStream(buffer_size), fd_(-1), port_(port), buffer_size_(buffer_size), buffer_(nullptr) {}
@@ -66,7 +68,15 @@
 }

 int HvSocketStream::commitBuffer(size_t size) {
-    return size == 0 ? 0 : writeFully(buffer_, size);
+    if (size == 0) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    const int result = writeFully(buffer_, size);
+    // What was sent is dead: the next transfer writes the buffer again before anything reads
+    // it. Pages past the first part go back when the guest reclaims page cache, so a buffer
+    // that once carried a large transfer does not keep it resident for the process's life.
+    dsa::releaseSparePages(buffer_, size);
+    return result;
 }

 int HvSocketStream::writeFully(const void* buffer, size_t size) {
